# Purpose
This file contains only generic engineering rules.
Project-specific product and implementation details are in:
- [Business Doc](./business-doc.mdc)
- [Specific Tech Doc](./specific-tech-doc.mdc)
- [Specific Data Model](./specific-data-model.md)

# Operating principles
- Open source or free solutions by default.
- Keep changes small, incremental, and verifiable.
- Type safety is mandatory.
- Use clear, semantic naming.
- Avoid over-engineering.
- Add comments only when logic is not obvious.
- Keep stable data in config, operational data in DB.

# Standard agent workflow
1. Read only the minimum context needed before planning.
2. Define a short plan focused on small tasks.
3. Implement the minimum useful change.
4. Verify according to impact (tests, lint, runtime checks).
5. Update impacted docs in the same change set.

# Code conventions
- Validate at the edge (controller or request boundary).
- Use early returns for auth and ownership checks.
- Separate HTTP transport concerns from domain logic.
- Avoid duplication: extract only when a pattern is stable.

# Security
- Never commit secrets or credentials.
- Never trust client input.
- Destructive actions require explicit authorization.
- Constrain uploads with validation (type, mime, size).

# Language standard
- Technical artifacts in English (code, names, tests, commits, technical messages).
- User-facing copy can be localized when required by the product.
- Conversation language follows the user's language.

# Documentation rules
- Update only truly impacted docs.
- Avoid duplication: one rule must have one source of truth.
- Keep text short, executable, and action-oriented.
- Ask for confirmation before changing general standards on request.

# Skills and OpenSpec
- If a skill applies, read its SKILL.md first.
- Use OpenSpec when work needs structured proposal/design/tasks.
- For small fixes or direct requests, do not force OpenSpec.
